  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "An entire school closed its doors in 1902 after decades of operation. Official state documents listed the institution one year, and blank space the next, leaving decades of student rosters and records behind.",
        "visual_query": "vintage brick school building exterior",
        "visual_fallback": "old brick building facade"
      },
      {
        "narration": "For decades, historians stared at government archives in upstate New York, hunting for the Delaware Literary Institute. Founded in 1835, it was a prominent secondary school with hundreds of enrolled students.",
        "visual_query": "historic nineteenth century american town",
        "visual_fallback": "old town street vintage"
      },
      {
        "narration": "What kind of bureaucratic phantom erases an entire campus from government books? Was it a massive cover-up, a lost funding scandal, or something far stranger hidden inside the state capital's filing cabinets?",
        "visual_query": "dusty antique archive room books",
        "visual_fallback": "old library stacks books"
      },
      {
        "narration": "By the early 1900s, increasing competition from public schools created financial pressures. The Institute surrendered its charter and leased its buildings for use as a public school, turning a century of education into history.",
        "visual_query": "turn of the century paperwork documents",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage parchment paper close"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Some of the physical structures still stand today in Franklin, New York, with surviving buildings now used by a professional theater company. How many other historical sites are hiding right in plain sight?",
        "visual_query": "small town brick community building",
        "visual_fallback": "historic town building exterior"
      }
    ],
